                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran examines the 2026 debate over whether rising U.S. Treasury rates ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â which climbed to 4.75% by August ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â should be dragging stock prices down. He finds that while corporate bond costs rose and bond prices fell, equity markets remained resilient because corporate earnings surged over 11% for both 2026 and 2027. His core argument is that interest rates are downstream of inflation expectations and real growth, not the Fed\u0027s policy knob ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â meaning the rate-stock relationship depends entirely on why rates are moving, not just that they are moving.",
                                                                                "take":  "Damodaran\u0027s framing cuts through a lot of market noise: investors who automatically sell stocks when rates rise are applying a mechanical rule to a fundamentally contextual question. In an environment where rates are rising because earnings and nominal GDP are genuinely strong ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â not because inflation is out of control ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â the equity risk premium may actually be compressing in a healthy way. For enterprise decision-makers, this is a useful filter: rate headlines are not actionable without understanding the growth signal embedded in them.",
                                                                                "isNew":  true
