                                                                                "summary":  "Brown profiles Ron Baron\u0027s Baron Capital, the top-performing mutual fund in America since 2003, tracing how $10 million in AUM grew to roughly $70 billion. The outsized returns were anchored by early conviction bets ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â Tesla purchased around $14 per split-adjusted share in 2014 and a $25 billion SpaceX position ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â held through enormous volatility. The post features an interview with Ron and Michael Baron on their investing philosophy: identify visionary founders, take concentrated positions, and maintain conviction across decades rather than quarters.",
                                                                                "take":  "The Baron case study is a useful stress test for how most institutional investors actually behave under volatility ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â and how most fail. The ability to hold Tesla through multiple 50%+ drawdowns or SpaceX through years of illiquidity is less a function of analysis than of organizational culture and incentive structure. For enterprise professionals evaluating long-duration technology bets ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â AI infrastructure, robotics, autonomous systems ÃƒÆ’Ã‚Â¢ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â‚¬Å¡Ã‚Â¬ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã‚Â the Baron model suggests the decisive variable is not picking the right company; it\u0027s building the internal governance that lets you hold it.",
